*.webm filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.mp4 filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.mov filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

# ...EXCEPT the video-benchmark fixtures, which are 2-4 KB each and are
# `include_bytes!`d into the binary. Under the blanket rule above, a checkout
# without git-lfs compiled the 130-byte POINTER TEXT into the binary as though
# it were an MP4: the video gate then failed or SKIPPED every MP4 leg — and a
# skipped leg reads far less alarming than a broken one. That made the build
# depend on LFS being installed and pulled, which for four kilobytes buys
# nothing. `provision_tests::each_clip_really_is_the_container_it_claims`
# checks the magic bytes and is what catches a regression here.
#
# The 6 MB assets/atlas-demo.mp4 is deliberately NOT exempted — it is what the
# blanket rule is for.
crates/atlas-plugin/assets/video/*.mp4 -filter -diff -merge -text
tests/fixtures/videos/*.mp4 -filter -diff -merge -text

# Append-style files that every feature PR touches (changelog entry, model-support
# row). Without this, each merge to main conflicts the other open PRs on these files.
# `merge=union` keeps both sides' added lines instead of emitting a conflict.
# (Union may duplicate a line if two PRs edit the exact same one — harmless, de-dup at release.)
CHANGELOG.md merge=union
README.md merge=union

# `ledger.rs` documents this and nothing declared it: two PR branches that each
# carry a harvested ledger line would otherwise CONFLICT on append-only files.
# Union keeps both sides' lines, and `Journey::deduplicated` collapses any
# duplicate on read — identity excludes `at`, so a replay converges.
governance/*.jsonl merge=union

# The bounded-FIFO archive index. Append-only for the same reason the ledgers
# are, and gets the same conflict-free merge: two harvests evicting different
# PRs concurrently produce two different tails of the same file, and union is
# exactly the right resolution. The header row is written once and unions
# idempotently with itself.
governance/archive.csv merge=union
